(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-2-cardiovascular-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Advanced Cardiovascular Examination Quiz 2 — PD2 Exam 2","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is Homan's sign elicited, and what is a positive result?","choices":["Forceful dorsiflexion, knee bent; pain behind it","Compression of both wrist arteries; delayed hand filling","Thumb pressure for two seconds; a persisting depression","Simultaneous radial and femoral palpation; a delay"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a test that checks for deep vein thrombosis, performed quickly and forcefully at the ankle. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"What usually causes a systolic click?","choices":["Pericarditis","Mitral valve prolapse","Aortic valve stenosis","Mitral stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — abnormal systolic ballooning of part of the valve into the left atrium, from leaflet redundancy and elongation of the chordae tendineae. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What should the examiner attend to about their hands?","choices":["Long nails for precise palpation","Warm hands and short nails","Gloved hands at all times","Cold hands to provoke vasoconstriction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside a quiet room and adequate lighting — small things that determine whether the patient stays relaxed enough for the findings to be real. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which valves are open during ventricular systole?","choices":["None of them","The pulmonic and aortic valves","The tricuspid and mitral valves","All four valves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary arteries and the left into the aorta, while the tricuspid and mitral valves are closed. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Where does the murmur of pulmonic stenosis radiate?","choices":["To the left axilla","To the right sternum and xiphoid","Toward the left shoulder and neck, if loud","To the carotids and down the left sternal border"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, from the pulmonic area. It is midsystolic, crescendo-decrescendo, medium and harsh, and no manoeuvres are listed for it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What should be remembered about palpitations?","choices":["They are the most common symptom of coronary disease","They are only significant if they last over an hour","They do not necessarily mean heart disease","They always indicate an arrhythmia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — anxious and hyperthyroid patients may report them. The instruction attached is to get an electrocardiogram and to reword your questions if needed. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How is the ankle brachial index calculated?","choices":["Brachial systolic over the higher ankle pressure","Lower of the two ankle pressures over the brachial systolic","Average of both ankle pressures over the average brachial","Higher ankle pressure over brachial systolic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, calculated for each leg and recorded to two decimal places. The normal range is 0.90 to 1.40. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What produces a pathologic S3?","choices":["Turbulence across a narrowed mitral valve","High filling pressures, abruptly decelerating inflow","Atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle","Sudden halting of a semilunar valve as it opens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — across the mitral valve at the end of the rapid filling phase. The image offered is blood slapping against the left ventricular wall. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which of these is a named cause of a murmur?","choices":["Inflammation of the pericardium","Reduced ventricular compliance","High left ventricular filling pressure","Ejection into a dilated chamber"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside flow across a partially obstructed valve, increased flow through a normal valve in a small child, regurgitant flow across an incompetent valve, and abnormal shunting to a lower pressure chamber. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which four diagnoses should always be considered with chest pain?","choices":["Angina, infarction, dissection, embolism","Pericarditis, pleurisy, oesophagitis and gastritis","Anxiety, hyperthyroidism, anaemia and pregnancy","Heart failure, valve disease, arrhythmia and cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and beyond them the task is distinguishing cardiovascular causes from pericardium, trachea, bronchi, parietal pleura, oesophagus, chest wall, gallbladder, stomach and neck. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What happens to mitral valve prolapse on squatting or leg raise?","choices":["It is moved later in systole","It becomes louder and earlier","It disappears entirely","It becomes a diastolic murmur"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the increased ventricular volume delays the point at which the redundant leaflet balloons into the atrium. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What are the findings of chronic venous insufficiency?","choices":["Cool skin with decreased pulses and no oedema","Foot pallor on elevation with dusky rubor","No pain, normal temperature, pitting oedema","Painful ulceration with thin shiny skin and hair loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the haemosiderin staining, thickened skin, normal pulses, and gangrene rare or absent. With brown pigmentation. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How is the carotid examined?","choices":["Auscultate first, then palpate one side at a time","Palpate both sides simultaneously to compare","Palpate first, then auscultate","Auscultate while the patient breathes normally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with the patient holding their breath, lateral to the trachea. Palpating both at once risks compromising cerebral flow. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"How do diastolic murmurs compare with systolic ones?","choices":["Always pathologic and always loud","Less common and more difficult to hear","More common and easier to hear","Equally common but louder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why the positioning and the choice of chest piece matter more for them than for the systolic murmurs. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How do aortic and pulmonic ejection sounds differ with respiration?","choices":["Both increase with inspiration","Both disappear with inspiration","The aortic does not vary; the pulmonic decreases with inspiration","The aortic decreases with inspiration; the pulmonic does not vary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and their locations differ too — aortic at the base and apex, pulmonic best in the second and third intercostal spaces. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What should a radial to femoral pulse lag prompt you to consider?","choices":["Chronic venous insufficiency","Mitral valve prolapse","Coarctation of the aorta","Deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, found by palpating the radial and femoral pulses simultaneously and noting a delay between them. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which sounds is the bell better for?","choices":["High-pitched sounds","Both equally","Only murmurs, not heart sounds","Low-pitched sounds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — S3, S4 and the murmur of mitral stenosis. It is applied lightly to the skin, ensuring no gaps, at the apex and moving medially along the lower sternal border. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where is the right ventricular impulse felt?","choices":["Fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Second intercostal space at the right sternal border","Over the carotid arteries","The 3rd to 5th left intercostal spaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and in pressure or volume overload it may extend to the subxiphoid process. Diameter is described as not useful for this impulse. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is a patient asked to perform a Valsalva?","choices":["Grip both hands tightly for thirty seconds","Lean forward and exhale completely","Bear down as if straining","Squat quickly from standing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with the patient supine and your hand on their mid-abdomen. The manoeuvre can also identify heart failure and pulmonary hypertension. Or push against your hand on their abdomen. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Where is the brachial pulse found?","choices":["At the lateral portion of the wrist","Behind the medial malleolus","Medial to the biceps tendon at the elbow","Lateral to the biceps tendon at the elbow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the radial pulse is found at the lateral portion of the wrist while the ulnar is medial. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What produces the second heart sound?","choices":["Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves","Atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle","Turbulent flow across a narrowed valve","Closure of the pulmonic and aortic valves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As the ventricles relax, ventricular pressure falls below arterial pressure and the semilunar valves close. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When does paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea typically occur?","choices":["Immediately on lying down","In the early morning on waking","Only during exertion","One to two hours after going to bed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — episodes of sudden dyspnoea and orthopnoea that wake the patient, prompting them to sit up, stand, or go to a window for air. Wheezing and coughing may accompany it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What causes a pericardial friction rub?","choices":["Reduced ventricular compliance","Inflammation of the pericardial sac","Turbulent flow across a stenotic valve","Regurgitant flow across an incompetent valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pericarditis. It is the one auscultatory finding here that crosses both phases of the cycle. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What is normal capillary refill time?","choices":["Two to three seconds","Under one second","Five to six seconds","It varies too much to have a normal value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, tested by pressing on the fingernail and noting how long the nail bed takes to become pink again. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the cardiac cycle?","choices":["The interval between S1 and S2","From the start of one heartbeat to the next","The period of ventricular contraction only","The period of ventricular relaxation only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — alternating periods of contraction and relaxation, divided into the two phases of systole and diastole. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What position brings out aortic regurgitation?","choices":["Sitting forward, breath held after exhaling","Left lateral decubitus with the bell","Supine while bearing down","Standing quickly from a squat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the same position is used for the pericardial friction rub — both are brought closer to the chest wall by it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What does a diffuse ventricular impulse indicate?","choices":["Ventricular hypertrophy from increased afterload","A transient rise in stroke volume","Constriction of the pericardium","Dilation from chronic volume overload"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — increased preload, with aortic or mitral regurgitation and cardiomyopathy as the examples. The impulse is displaced left and possibly downward. That is increased preload. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why is the murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy singled out as important to learn?","choices":["Because it radiates to the carotids","So a sports physical is not signed off incorrectly","Because it is the commonest murmur in adults","Because it always produces a thrill"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is high-pitched, crescendo-decrescendo and midsystolic at the left lower sternal border, and missing it in a young athlete has obvious consequences. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which questions help characterise oedema?","choices":["Do shoes or rings get tight? Eyelids puffy?","How far can you walk before the pain stops you?","How many pillows do you sleep on?","Does the sensation feel like skipping or fluttering?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, focusing on location, timing and setting — and whether it is worse in the morning or the evening, which points toward different causes. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Where is the point of maximal impulse normally found?","choices":["Fifth space, at or just medial to midclavicular","Second intercostal space at the right sternal border","Fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border","Sixth intercostal space at the anterior axillary line"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it identifies the LEFT BORDER of the heart — which is why a change in cardiac size or shape moves it. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"}]};
